specs/cli-jev/003-cli-jev-workflow-integration/017-deem-search-narrowing-arm/spec.md:136:| REQ-003 | **Both baselines are scored at track level on identical rows.** Lookup: `lookup()` from `lookup-trigger-index.mjs` with limit 0 on the committed index. The pick is the track of the first row in the lookup's own order that has a score above 0 and a path under `specs/`. Ripgrep: the question's distinct normalized tokens of 3 or more characters, each run through the path-only recipe of `lib/rg-lane.mjs` over the `specs` root. Each file scores the number of distinct tokens it matches. The pick is the track of the top file, ties going to the track with the most files at that score, then to track name order. No scoring candidate is an abstention and counts as a miss. The better of the two accuracies is the baseline. The report prints the index `manifestHash` |
specs/cli-jev/003-cli-jev-workflow-integration/017-deem-search-narrowing-arm/spec.md:137:| REQ-004 | **The keep rule is fixed before any model run, applies per backend column and is the rule phase 009 cites.** Inputs: the K kept rows (REQ-002). The baseline method is whichever of the lookup and ripgrep names the right track on more kept rows, the lookup on a tie. A column's M measured rows are those whose three calls each returned a submitted key after REQ-008's handling. On a measured row the backend is right when its modal pick is the row's track, and `unstable` or `none` counts as wrong. The baseline method is right when its pick is the row's track, and an abstention counts as wrong. Over the M rows, A counts the rows the backend gets right and B the rows the baseline method gets right. W counts the rows only the backend gets right and L the rows only the baseline method gets right. F sums each row's non-modal picks, 3 minus the count of its most common pick. `verdict <backend>: keep` needs all four conditions below, checked in this order. Otherwise the script prints `verdict <backend>: stop (<reason>)`, naming the first that failed. Coverage: `10*M >= 9*K`, else `stop (coverage)`. Margin: `10*(A-B) >= M`, a gain of at least 10 points, else `stop (margin)`. Sign test: the exact one-sided binomial p, the chance of W or more successes in W+L fair trials, is below 0.05, with p = 1 when W+L is 0, else `stop (sign test)`. Flips: `10*F <= 3*M`, a flip rate of at most 0.10, else `stop (flips)`. Counts stay integers and p is computed exactly, so no rounding decides a verdict. Before any call the script prints `margin: 0.10` and one `keep rule:` line naming all four thresholds. When the baseline method is right on more than 90 percent of kept rows, a 10-point gain cannot fit, so the zero-call run prints `no headroom` and neither arm calls. The verdict line prints on stdout and in that column of `report.json`, with K, M, A, B, W, L, F, p and what the column was measured on (REQ-009). A stopped arm prints no verdict (REQ-008). Only a `verdict deem: keep` from a live `--deem --out <dir>` run against the served instance is the operator's keep that unlocks phase 009 (parent goal D4). (Superseded 2026-09-29: 009 unlocks once 008 is Complete, parent D4 amended. A keep still decides whether a pick may be served, which needs a later phase.) A stub, fake-server or vitest keep never counts. Changing this rule after the first model run is an amendment that voids every earlier verdict |
specs/cli-jev/003-cli-jev-workflow-integration/017-deem-search-narrowing-arm/spec.md:141:| REQ-013 | **No key in any file, and nothing extra leaves the machine.** The script never reads, stores, logs or passes a key and holds no key literal or key variable name. `jev` resolves its own key, and `cli-deem` takes none. `grep -nE 'API_KEY\|TYPESAFE\|Bearer\|Authorization' score-track-narrowing.mjs` returns no match, and no spawned argument list carries a key. A Jev request carries only the question text on stdin, the one fixed `-q` instruction of REQ-007 and the 17 option keys and descriptions as `-o` pairs |
specs/cli-jev/003-cli-jev-workflow-integration/017-deem-search-narrowing-arm/spec.md:150:| REQ-010 | **The paraphrase probes are reported, never decisive.** For each of the 20 Latin paraphrase rows in `semantic-probes.json`, the gold is the set of tracks holding a scoring `specs/` row for the matching exact query on the fresh index. Probes with an empty gold set are counted and skipped. The line prints hits for the lookup, ripgrep and each model column that ran, where a pick inside the gold set is a hit |
specs/cli-jev/003-cli-jev-workflow-integration/017-deem-search-narrowing-arm/spec.md:151:| REQ-011 | **Tests cover every changed surface.** `score-track-narrowing.vitest.ts` exits 0 with a happy path and one edge case each: the test set builds stratified rows and drops a leaking description, the lookup baseline picks a track and ignores the question's own folder, the ripgrep baseline picks the track of the file matching the most distinct tokens, the default run logs no stub call and prints `no headroom` on a saturated fixture, the Deem gate passes a fake health and a stub backend skips with byte-identical output, the Jev gate passes a stub whose `auth status --provider P` exits 0 and prints `jev arm skipped: no credential` when it exits 3, and the verdict prints `keep` on stub answers and `stop (margin)` on a small gain. Also: a Deem exit 4 whose recheck shows a new pair prints `deem arm stopped: model commit changed mid-run`, a stored pair that differs prints `requalify: model commit changed`, a Jev exit 3 after the gate prints `jev arm stopped: key rejected`, and in a stub run that passes the Jev gate every logged `jev` call carries the same `--provider` value. A last case covers REQ-004's coverage condition: with 2 of 10 kept rows unmeasured and every measured pick right, the verdict prints `stop (coverage)`. That is at least 18 cases |
specs/cli-jev/003-cli-jev-workflow-integration/017-deem-search-narrowing-arm/spec.md:202:- **SC-001**: Before any model call, the operator reads how many clean questions each track has, how often ripgrep and the lookup name the right track and whether a 10-point gain is reachable.
specs/cli-jev/003-cli-jev-workflow-integration/017-deem-search-narrowing-arm/spec.md:215:| Stale premise | Plan step 1 names `--manifest`, `--diagnostics` and `--variants` because the generator wrote tracked fixtures by default (`generate-trigger-index.mjs:76-79`). Since 010's `a0368b4a58`, `--out` alone puts every sidecar beside it (`:35-37`, `resolveArtifactPaths` at `:351-362`), and `--check` compares an index with the corpus and writes nothing | Low | The extra flags stay harmless, so plan step 1 is unchanged. The build may add `--check` exiting 0 as a second freshness receipt |
specs/cli-jev/003-cli-jev-workflow-integration/017-deem-search-narrowing-arm/spec.md:240:- **NFR-P01**: The zero-call run finishes in one sitting on this Mac and prints its wall time. The ripgrep baseline caches each token's result across questions. The build measured the zero-call run at 1,944 s alone and 2,453 s under load.
.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/lib/frontmatter.mjs:128: * this one only reads, and each refusal here silently narrows retrieval instead.
.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/rg-wrapper.mjs:12://    `--files-with-matches` and `--count`, and ripgrep resolves the conflict by
.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/rg-wrapper.mjs:16:// 2. It never treats ripgrep's output order as relevance. Ripgrep supplies
.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/rg-wrapper.mjs:26:// the shared retrieval lane, which is what keeps two spellings of one recipe
.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/rg-wrapper.mjs:155: * roots as the shared retrieval lane's. Order differs by design — the wrapper
.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/rg-wrapper.mjs:157: * is not meaningful to ripgrep, but a divergence in the flag *set* would mean
.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/rg-wrapper.mjs:331: * @returns {number} Process exit code, mapped from ripgrep's own.
